  "full_response": "Here’s a **comprehensive comparison** of **Amazon Web Services (AWS), Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud Platform (GCP)** across **20+ service categories**, including **pros and cons** for each provider.\n\n---\n\n## **1. Compute Services**\n### **AWS: EC2 (Elastic Compute Cloud)**\n✅ **Pros:**\n- Largest instance variety (300+ types, including ARM-based Graviton).\n- Spot Instances for cost savings (up to 90% discount).\n- Auto Scaling with granular control.\n- Strong hybrid cloud support (Outposts, Local Zones).\n\n❌ **Cons:**\n- Complex pricing (reserved instances, savings plans).\n- Steeper learning curve for beginners.\n\n### **Azure: Virtual Machines (VMs)**\n✅ **Pros:**\n- Deep Windows/Linux integration (especially Windows Server).\n- Azure Hybrid Benefit (reuse existing Windows licenses).\n- Burstable VMs (B-series) for cost efficiency.\n- Strong enterprise adoption (Microsoft ecosystem).\n\n❌ **Cons:**\n- Fewer instance types than AWS.\n- Some features require PowerShell/CLI.\n\n### **GCP: Compute Engine**\n✅ **Pros:**\n- **Sustained-use discounts** (automatic cost savings).\n- **Live migration** of VMs (no downtime during host maintenance).\n- **Custom machine types** (optimize CPU/RAM).\n- Strong Kubernetes integration (GKE).\n\n❌ **Cons:**\n- Smaller global footprint than AWS/Azure.\n- Fewer enterprise features (e.g., no native hybrid cloud like Outposts).\n\n---\n\n## **2. Serverless Computing**\n### **AWS: Lambda**\n✅ **Pros:**\n- **Largest language support** (Python, Node.js, Java, Go, Ruby, .NET, Rust).\n- **Longest max execution time (15 min)**.\n- **Event-driven integrations** (S3, DynamoDB, API Gateway).\n- **Provisioned Concurrency** (reduces cold starts).\n\n❌ **Cons:**\n- **Cold starts** can be slow (~100ms–2s).\n- **Complex pricing** (requests + compute time).\n\n### **Azure: Azure Functions**\n✅ **Pros:**\n- **Deep Microsoft integration** (Office 365, Power Platform).\n- **Durable Functions** (stateful workflows).\n- **Consumption & Premium plans** (",
